  "summary": "San Mateo's Parks and Recreation Department signed a $91,000 contract with Verkada to expand surveillance cameras in city parks, connecting them to the police department's new Real Time Information Center. Because contracts were under $100,000 and the city lacks a CCOPS ordinance, the expansion did not require council approval or public comment; an EFF researcher warned about lack of oversight, citing past ALPR data-sharing violations in the Bay Area.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "the Parks and Recreation Department expanded its own, lesser-known surveillance camera program with a new $91,000 contract with San Mateo-based Verkada.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "the city doesn't have a CCOPS ordinance, and because its contracts for the cameras were technically under $100,000, they didn't require explicit City Council approval",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "At least 10 ALPR data sharing violations occurred across several Bay Area cities and counties",
      "paragraph_idx": 22
    }
  ],
